= Wave 3 — The Throne of Titans

== The Story

#storybox[
  The old empires have fallen, and the great powers that remain are of a different order — titans girded for a contest that ends only when one rules the world. Every alliance is a lie waiting for the right moment, every border a battlefield waiting for the right general. The throne is empty. The world watches to see who will take it.
]

== Game Setup

Monarch is the sandbox, Immortal is the proving ground, Deity for the brave. Continents, Fractal, and Pangaea all work — pick your favorite shape. Standard to Large maps give titans room; Epic or Marathon speed stretches the game into a long campaign. Above all, play ironman: no reloads, no second chances. The throne belongs to whoever wins with the hand they are dealt.

== Victory Race Management

Commit to one victory type early and let every later decision serve it — and deny rivals theirs: a diplomatic win dies when you hold the largest population and keep rivals from the Secretary General's seat at the United Nations and the Resident's at the Apostolic Palace; a cultural win dies when one of the three legendary cities is taken before the culture completes. A rival allowed two open victory tracks will take one.

#warn[
  At the United Nations, only the Secretary General wins a diplomatic victory, and only with a majority of the population-weighted votes cast. Keep your population on top and rival friendships short.
]

== Advanced Warfare

Modern war is won from the air and sea before a soldier marches. Fighters win the sky and intercept enemy bombers; bombers and later stealth bombers flatten cities; carriers project it across oceans. A naval blockade cuts a rival's trade routes and starves their gold. Nuclear weapons end wars in a turn, but the Manhattan Project announces your intent and every launch poisons world opinion — expect broken alliances and a united front. The kinder sword is vassalage: batter a foe until they capitulate — their land and population count toward domination while they fight your wars.

#info[
  Tactical nukes are cheaper and launch from ships; ICBMs reach any tile. Both carry the same diplomatic fallout — once you use them, you own the aftermath.
]

== Economy at Scale

Trailing in tech is a position, not a fate. Trade what you have for what you lack, run a spy economy to steal whole branches from the leaders, and bulb the rest with great scientists. Open borders with everyone who will sign, and let foreign trade routes carry your commerce. In the corporation endgame, Sid's Sushi Co feeds cities and adds culture, Mining Inc turns coal and iron into hammers, and Aluminum Co secures the resource. Keep the great people flowing: settle some, bulb some, and hold pairs for golden ages.

== Micromanagement

The last ten percent of difficulty lives in the details. Reassign tiles by hand whenever a city grows — the governor's defaults serve no plan. Then choose the specialists: scientists in science cities, engineers in production cities, merchants in commerce cities. Time wonders to land during a golden age so doubled production applies to the whole build, and chain golden ages back to back when you have the people for it.

#tip[
  Whipping converts population into hammers immediately — thirty per citizen at Normal speed, more on slower speeds — at the cost of one unhappy citizen per whipped pop for ten turns. Worth it whenever that building or unit matters more than the anger; never worth it when it stalls a city's growth curve.
]

== City Specialization

Every city earns one label and one job. A science city stacks libraries and universities and runs scientists. A production city works every mine and runs engineers, building the army for everyone. A great person farm piles on national wonders like the National Epic and runs as many specialists as food allows. A commerce city works cottages, then a market and a bank, and finances the empire. Cities that do all of this do all of it badly.

== Endgame Diplomacy

The endgame is a courtroom as much as a battlefield. Once defensive pacts unlock, sign one with a partner who likes you — an attack on either of you is a war on both. At the United Nations, votes are population, so keep yours highest and vote down resolutions that could crown a rival. At the Apostolic Palace, watch who built it and in which holy city, then weigh the religion's influence against its resident. And watch the scoreboard: a rival pulling ahead on land, population, and wonders is climbing toward your throne.

